---
import DocPage from '../layouts/DocPage.astro';
import { stats } from '../data/stats';

const title = 'Tidewave MCP';
const description =
  'Use Tidewave MCP with phxagents and Phoenix across Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Amp, Pi, and dsh — runtime tools, client setup, and local fallbacks.';
const pageUrl = 'https://phxagents.dev/tidewave-mcp/';

// Structured data: Breadcrumb + a TechArticle for the Tidewave MCP guide, plus
// an FAQPage so AI answer engines can extract the "what / how" directly. Mirrors
// the pattern used on skill/agent detail pages.
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [
    {
      '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
      itemListElement: [
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'phxagents', item: 'https://phxagents.dev/' },
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Tidewave MCP', item: pageUrl },
      ],
    },
    {
      '@type': 'TechArticle',
      '@id': `${pageUrl}#article`,
      headline: 'Tidewave MCP for Phoenix',
      name: 'Tidewave MCP for Phoenix',
      description,
      url: pageUrl,
      inLanguage: 'en',
      author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Oliver Kriška', url: 'https://github.com/oliver-kriska' },
      publisher: { '@id': 'https://phxagents.dev/#org' },
      isPartOf: { '@id': 'https://phxagents.dev/#website' },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'FAQPage',
      '@id': `${pageUrl}#faq`,
      mainEntity: [
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'What is Tidewave MCP?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text:
              'Tidewave MCP is a Model Context Protocol server (from Dashbit) that exposes a running Phoenix application’s runtime to AI editors and coding agents — live SQL queries, code evaluation, Ecto schema and source lookup, logs, and version-exact hex docs. phxagents teaches supported runtimes to prefer these tools when the client exposes them, while preserving local fallbacks.',
          },
        },
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'How do I use Tidewave MCP with phxagents?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text:
              'Install the phxagents edition for your runtime, add Tidewave to the Phoenix app, and register the project-specific HTTP endpoint with your client. Claude Code, Codex, and OpenCode have documented registration paths; Tidewave remains externally configured for Amp, Pi, and dsh. Every adapted workflow retains a local fallback when the connection is absent.',
          },
        },
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'Is Tidewave MCP safe to use in production?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text:
              'No. phxagents treats Tidewave as dev-only. Its tools evaluate code and run SQL against the live runtime, so the plugin never uses them in production contexts and avoids shared dev servers holding copies of production data.',
          },
        },
      ],
    },
  ],
};
---

<DocPage title={title} description={description} active="docs">
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)} />

  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>Tidewave MCP for Phoenix</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      <strong>Tidewave MCP</strong> gives your AI editor runtime intelligence for a running Phoenix
      app — live SQL, code evaluation, Ecto schema introspection, and version-exact hex docs over the
      Model Context Protocol. <a href="/install/">phxagents</a> ships a dedicated
      <a href="/skills/tidewave-integration/">Tidewave integration skill</a> in every v3 edition, so
      the agent can prefer runtime evidence over shell-based guesses when its client exposes the connection.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="docs-body">
    <h2>What is Tidewave MCP?</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="https://tidewave.ai" rel="noopener">Tidewave</a> (from Dashbit, the team behind Elixir
      and Livebook) exposes a running Phoenix application&rsquo;s runtime to AI editors and coding
      agents through an <strong>MCP server</strong>. Instead of reading static files and guessing,
      the agent can query the live database, evaluate code in the running app, look up Ecto schemas
      and source locations, read logs, and fetch documentation pinned to <em>your</em>
      <code>mix.lock</code> versions.
    </p>
    <p>
      Claude Code receives the full Tidewave-aware plugin; Amp, Codex, Pi, OpenCode and DeepSeek Harness receive the
      same <a href="/skills/tidewave-integration/"><code>tidewave-integration</code> skill</a> in their
      generated editions. Installing any edition exposes no fixed-port server and registers no MCP
      endpoint. When a client makes Tidewave tools available in a dev session, the skill can use them;
      adapted workflows keep a complete local fallback when it cannot.
    </p>

    <h2 id="tools">Runtime tools phxagents uses</h2>
    <p>The names below are how Claude Code exposes the tools. Other MCP clients may label them differently, but the capabilities and local fallbacks are the same:</p>
    <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Tidewave runtime tools">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Claude Code tool name</th><th>Fallback</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Version-exact hex docs</td><td><code>mcp__tidewave__get_docs</code></td><td><code>web_fetch hexdocs.pm/…</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Evaluate code in the app</td><td><code>mcp__tidewave__project_eval</code></td><td><code>mix run -e "…"</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Run a SQL query</td><td><code>mcp__tidewave__execute_sql_query</code></td><td><code>psql $DATABASE_URL</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Find a source location</td><td><code>mcp__tidewave__get_source_location</code></td><td><code>grep -rn "defmodule"</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>List Ecto schemas</td><td><code>mcp__tidewave__get_ecto_schemas</code></td><td>Read <code>lib/*/schemas/</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Read runtime logs</td><td><code>mcp__tidewave__get_logs</code></td><td><code>tail -f log/dev.log</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 id="setup">Setup &amp; detection</h2>
    <p>
      Add Tidewave to your Phoenix app (see <a href="https://tidewave.ai" rel="noopener">tidewave.ai</a>),
      then confirm the MCP endpoint is reachable in development:
    </p>
    <div class="code-block">
      <div class="code-head">
        <span>Check the Tidewave MCP endpoint</span>
        <button class="copy" data-copy>Copy</button>
      </div>
      <pre>curl -s http://localhost:4000/tidewave/mcp \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '&lbrace;"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"ping"&rbrace;'</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
      Exposing that HTTP server and registering it with an MCP client are separate steps. phxagents
      never silently registers a fixed port. Replace <code>$PORT</code> below with the port used by
      the current Phoenix project.
    </p>

    <h3>Claude Code</h3>
    <div class="code-block">
      <div class="code-head">
        <span>Register and verify</span>
        <button class="copy" data-copy>Copy</button>
      </div>
      <pre>claude mcp add --transport http tidewave \
  http://localhost:$PORT/tidewave/mcp

# Then run /mcp inside Claude Code</pre>
    </div>

    <h3>Codex</h3>
    <div class="code-block">
      <div class="code-head">
        <span>Register and inspect</span>
        <button class="copy" data-copy>Copy</button>
      </div>
      <pre>codex mcp add tidewave --url http://localhost:$PORT/tidewave/mcp
codex mcp list</pre>
    </div>
    <p>Start Codex and use <code>/mcp</code> to verify the tools are connected; <code>codex mcp list</code> confirms configuration only.</p>

    <h3>OpenCode</h3>
    <p>Add the endpoint to the project&rsquo;s <code>opencode.json</code> or the global <code>~/.config/opencode/opencode.json</code>:</p>
    <div class="code-block">
      <div class="code-head">
        <span>opencode.json</span>
        <button class="copy" data-copy>Copy</button>
      </div>
      <pre>&lbrace;
  "mcp": &lbrace;
    "tidewave": &lbrace;
      "type": "remote",
      "url": "http://localhost:$PORT/tidewave/mcp",
      "enabled": true
    &rbrace;
  &rbrace;
&rbrace;</pre>
    </div>

    <p>
      <strong>Amp and Pi:</strong> the generated package does not configure Tidewave. Treat MCP as
      an external runtime capability and follow your client&rsquo;s current setup. The skills remain usable
      without it. See the <a href="/compatibility/">runtime capability matrix</a> for the support boundary.
    </p>

    <h2 id="safety">Dev only — never in production</h2>
    <p>
      Tidewave tools evaluate code and run SQL against the live runtime, so phxagents enforces a
      hard rule: <strong>Tidewave is dev-only.</strong> The plugin never uses these tools in
      production contexts and avoids shared dev servers that hold copies of production data. SQL is
      treated as read-heavy — <code>execute_sql_query</code> for <code>SELECT</code>s, with care
      around mutations.
    </p>

    <h2>Learn more</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/skills/tidewave-integration/">tidewave-integration skill</a> — the full reference phxagents loads on demand</li>
      <li><a href="/install/">Install phxagents</a> — add {stats.skills} skills to your runtime; Claude Code also gets {stats.agents} agents and hooks</li>
      <li><a href="https://github.com/oliver-kriska/claude-elixir-phoenix/blob/main/plugins/elixir-phoenix/skills/tidewave-integration/SKILL.md" rel="noopener">SKILL.md source on GitHub</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://tidewave.ai" rel="noopener">tidewave.ai</a> — the Tidewave runtime intelligence product</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</DocPage>

<style>
  .page-head h1 {
    font-size: clamp(28px, 3.4vw, 38px);
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    margin: 0 0 8px;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--fg);
  }
  .page-head .lede {
    margin: 0 0 32px;
    color: var(--fg-soft);
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    max-width: 640px;
    padding-bottom: 24px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .page-head .lede a {
    color: var(--accent-fg);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--accent) 40%, transparent);
    text-underline-offset: 2px;
  }
  .docs-body { max-width: 720px; }
  .docs-body h2 {
    margin: 40px 0 14px;
    padding-top: 24px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    font-size: 20px;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--fg);
  }
  .docs-body h2:first-of-type { padding-top: 0; border-top: 0; margin-top: 0; }
  .docs-body h3 {
    margin: 24px 0 10px;
    color: var(--fg);
    font-size: 16px;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .docs-body p {
    margin: 0 0 14px;
    color: var(--fg-soft);
    line-height: 1.65;
    font-size: 14.5px;
  }
  .docs-body ul {
    margin: 0 0 16px 0;
    padding-left: 22px;
    color: var(--fg-soft);
    font-size: 14.5px;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }
  .docs-body li { margin: 4px 0; }
  .docs-body a {
    color: var(--accent-fg);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--accent) 40%, transparent);
    text-underline-offset: 2px;
  }
  .docs-body a:hover { color: var(--accent); }
  /* Scroll the table horizontally on narrow viewports instead of letting the
     non-wrapping mcp__tidewave__* tool names overflow the page. */
  .table-wrap {
    margin: 14px 0 22px;
    overflow-x: auto;
    -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }
  .docs-body table {
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 540px;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 13.5px;
  }
  .docs-body table code { white-space: nowrap; }
  .docs-body th, .docs-body td {
    padding: 10px 14px;
    text-align: left;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    vertical-align: top;
  }
  .docs-body th {
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: 11.5px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    background: var(--surface-2);
    color: var(--muted);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
  }
  .docs-body tr:last-child td { border-bottom: 0; }
</style>
